    WHEN `sch 2 part 2 none of the descriptions` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is of a description specified in Part 2 of that Schedule" (s 948(3)(c) hands the question to the list; ruling of 2026-11-05 (a))
    WHERE
        `the defined meaning` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the person or body meets the meaning the description gives to a term`
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the description is a disclosure about, or for, a person or body of a kind the paragraph defines in its sub-paragraph (2), and the person or body is not of that kind (supplied from the sub-paragraph's function)
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the sub-paragraph's meaning rests on another Act, or on a designation made outside the sources, which is not known

§§ `The sub-paragraphs that define a term used in a description (para 40(2), 52, 53; Jersey 12, 13; Guernsey 7, 8; Isle of Man 16, 17; General 2(2))`

-- The three terms the Schedule borrows from another Act whole (A 40(2) from the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, E 2(2) from the
-- Companies Act 1989) are inputs: JUST TRUE or JUST FALSE is the borrowed definition's answer, NOTHING is "not known".

-- src:19200 | (2) In sub-paragraph (1) “ recognised investment exchange ” , “recognised clearing house” and “recognised CSD” ” have the same meaning as in section 285 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000.
@ref sch 2 part 2(A) para 40(2) Recognised investment exchange, recognised clearing house, recognised CSD (src:19200)
GIVEN `the body is a recognised investment exchange, a recognised clearing house or a recognised CSD within the meaning of section 285 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 2 part 2(A) para 40(2) the body is a recognised investment exchange, a recognised clearing house or a recognised CSD` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the body is a recognised investment exchange, a recognised clearing house or a recognised CSD within the meaning of section 285 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "have the same meaning as in section 285 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000" (the borrowed definition says no, supplied as an input)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 is not among the sources

-- src:19319 | (2) In sub-paragraph (1) “ overseas regulatory authority ” and “ regulatory functions ” have the same meaning as in section 82 of the Companies Act 1989 (assistance for overseas regulatory authorities).
@ref sch 2 part 2(E) para 2(2) Overseas regulatory authority and regulatory functions (src:19319)
GIVEN `the body is an overseas regulatory authority exercising regulatory functions within the meaning of section 82 of the Companies Act 1989` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 2 part 2(E) para 2(2) the body is an overseas regulatory authority exercising regulatory functions` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the body is an overseas regulatory authority exercising regulatory functions within the meaning of section 82 of the Companies Act 1989`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "have the same meaning as in section 82 of the Companies Act 1989" (the borrowed definition says no, supplied as an input)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Companies Act 1989 is not among the sources

-- Para 52(3): until section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007 comes into force, a substituted paragraph (a) applies. Whether section 18 is in
-- force is a fact the person supplies (the text points to another Act and states no date).
-- src:19222 | (3) Until the coming into force of section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007, the following is substituted for paragraph (a) of sub-paragraph (2) above—
-- src:19223 | (a) a solicitor or barrister in England and Wales,
@ref sch 2 part 2(A) para 52(3) Until section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007 comes into force (src:19222-19223)
GIVEN `section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007 has come into force` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 2 part 2(A) para 52(3) the substituted paragraph (a) applies` MEANS
    IF `section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007 has come into force`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Until the coming into force of section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007, the following is substituted for paragraph (a) of sub-paragraph (2) above" (an "until" bar that has ended: ruling of 2026-11-05)
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:19217 | (2) In sub-paragraph (1) “ lawyer ” means—
-- src:19218 | (a) a person who for the purposes of the Legal Services Act 2007 (c. 29) is an authorised person in relation to an activity that constitutes a reserved legal activity (within the meaning of that Act),
-- src:19219 | (b) a solicitor or barrister in Northern Ireland,
-- src:19220 | (c) a solicitor or advocate in Scotland, or
-- src:19221 | (d) a person who is a member, and entitled to practise as such, of a legal profession regulated in a jurisdiction outside the United Kingdom.
@ref sch 2 part 2(A) para 52(2) Lawyer (src:19217-19221)
GIVEN `section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007 has come into force` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an authorised person in relation to an activity that constitutes a reserved legal activity (within the meaning of the Legal Services Act 2007)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a solicitor or barrister in England and Wales` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a solicitor or barrister in Northern Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a solicitor or advocate in Scotland` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a member, and entitled to practise as such, of a legal profession regulated in a jurisdiction outside the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 2 part 2(A) para 52(2) the person is a lawyer` MEANS
    IF       `limb (a)`
         OR  `the person is a solicitor or barrister in Northern Ireland`
         OR  `the person is a solicitor or advocate in Scotland`
         OR  `the person is a member, and entitled to practise as such, of a legal profession regulated in a jurisdiction outside the United Kingdom`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "In sub-paragraph (1) “ lawyer ” means— (a) a person who for the purposes of the Legal Services Act 2007 ... is an authorised person in relation to an activity that constitutes a reserved legal activity ..., (b) a solicitor or barrister in Northern Ireland, (c) a solicitor or advocate in Scotland, or (d) a person who is a member, and entitled to practise as such, of a legal profession regulated in a jurisdiction outside the United Kingdom"
    WHERE
        `limb (a)` MEANS
            IF `sch 2 part 2(A) para 52(3) the substituted paragraph (a) applies` `section 18 of the Legal Services Act 2007 has come into force` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN `the person is a solicitor or barrister in England and Wales`
            ELSE `the person is an authorised person in relation to an activity that constitutes a reserved legal activity (within the meaning of the Legal Services Act 2007)`

-- src:19226 | (2) In sub-paragraph (1) “ public servant ” means—
-- src:19227 | (a) an officer or employee of the Crown, or
-- src:19228 | (b) an officer or employee of any public or other authority for the time being designated for the purposes of this paragraph by the Secretary of State by order subject to negative resolution procedure.
@ref sch 2 part 2(A) para 53(2) Public servant (src:19226-19228)
GIVEN `the person is an officer or employee of the Crown` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an officer or employee of a public or other authority designated for the purposes of this paragraph by the Secretary of State by order subject to negative resolution procedure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 2 part 2(A) para 53(2) the person is a public servant` MEANS
    IF       `the person is an officer or employee of the Crown`
         OR  `the person is an officer or employee of a public or other authority designated for the purposes of this paragraph by the Secretary of State by order subject to negative resolution procedure`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "In sub-paragraph (1) “ public servant ” means— (a) an officer or employee of the Crown, or (b) an officer or employee of any public or other authority for the time being designated for the purposes of this paragraph by the Secretary of State by order subject to negative resolution procedure"

-- src:19246 | (a) “ solicitor ” means a person who has been admitted as a solicitor under the Advocates and Solicitors (Jersey) Law 1997;
